# 2025-10-08 23:36:20.886635
+You are generating a gold evaluation set for a cyber threat intelligence system named HEVA (Agent B). 
+HEVA supports two tasks:
+(1) Semantic search over short intel blurbs stored in Qdrant.
+(2) Named-entity extraction (NER) over the same blurbs.
+
+Output 60 examples as JSON Lines (one JSON object per line) with this schema:
+{
+  "doc_id": "<uuid-like or short id>",
+  "text": "<one short intel blurb (1-3 sentences)>",
+  "entities": [
+    {"start": <int>, "end": <int>, "label": "<one of: ThreatActor, Malware, CVE, Tool, TTP, IOC_IP, IOC_DOMAIN, IOC_HASH, Product, Vendor, Version>"}
+  ],
+  "queries": ["<query that should match this doc>", "..."],   // 1-3 queries
+  "tags": ["controlled", "source:synthetic", "difficulty:<easy|med|hard>"]
+}
+
+Requirements:
+- Balance the set roughly across labels above. Each example should have at least one entity.
+- Include 10 negative/ambiguous examples with no true entities (entities=[]), but still realistic CTI text.
+- Include obfuscations: leetspeak (e.g., ph1sh1ng), dotted indicators (1[.]2[.]3[.]4), defanged URLs, mixed case, Unicode homoglyphs, and truncated or malformed CVEs (e.g., CVE-2023-12xx).
+- Mix vendors/products (e.g., Cisco, Fortinet, Microsoft Exchange), higher-level TTPs (e.g., credential dumping), and tools (e.g., Cobalt Strike).
+- IOC formats: IPv4, domains, SHA256 hashes.
+- Threat actors: both known-style names (“CozyBear”, “APT28”) and generic (“unknown actor”).
+- For each example, ensure "entities" have correct character offsets over "text" (Python slicing text[start:end]).
+- For each example, "queries" should be natural phrases that SHOULD retrieve the doc via semantic similarity, e.g., "IronWind phishing lures", "CVE 2023 Fortinet ssl-vpn", etc.
+- Make texts concise and varied. Avoid repeating templates.
+
+Output only valid JSONL, no prose, no markdown.
+

